# 1. What is a process in Linux?
A process is a program that is currently running (executing) in the Linux operating system.

Every process has a unique Process ID (PID).
The Linux kernel manages all processes.
A process uses system resources such as CPU, memory, and files while it is running.

Example:
When you open a web browser, text editor, or run a command like ls, Linux creates a new process.

# 2. What is a PID (Process ID), and why is it important?
Answer:
A PID (Process ID) is a unique number assigned by the Linux operating system to every running process. It helps the system identify and manage each process.

> Why is it important?
To identify a running process.
To monitor process status.
To stop or kill a process.
To troubleshoot process-related issues.
> Example Commands
// ps -ef
(Shows all running processes with their PIDs.)
// kill <PID>
(Stops a process using its PID.)


# 3. How do you check all running processes in Linux?
Answer:
To check all running processes in Linux, I use the ps -ef command. It displays all running processes along with details such as the user, PID, CPU time, and command. For a real-time view of running processes, I use the top command.

> Commands
// ps -ef
(Shows all running processes.)
// top
(Shows running processes in real time.)

# 4. What is the difference between ps, top, and htop commands?
Answer:
ps – Shows a snapshot of the currently running processes.
top – Shows live (real-time) running processes and updates continuously.
htop – An interactive and user-friendly version of top with colors, scrolling, and easier process management.

> Commands
// ps -ef
(Shows a snapshot of all running processes.)
// top
(Shows processes in real time.)
// htop
(Shows an interactive real-time process monitor. It may need to be installed first.)


# 5. A server's CPU usage suddenly becomes 100%. How would you identify the process causing the issue?
Answer:
First, I use the top or htop command to monitor CPU usage in real time. Then I identify the process with the highest CPU usage by checking its PID and process name. If needed, I use ps -p <PID> -f to get more details about that process.

> Commands
// top
or
// htop
To get details of a specific process:
// ps -p <PID> -f
If the process is unnecessary or stuck:
// kill <PID>
or
// kill -9 <PID>

